# Resolution: Diagnose EvE engine anomalies — MCTS non-checkmate & zero castling

## Validation Checklist
- [x] Verify root cause of Issue A is demonstrated from actual code paths (not speculation)
- [x] Verify game-termination rules (50-move / repetition / ply cap) behavior in EvE mode is confirmed from code
- [x] Verify root cause of Issue B (no castling) is demonstrated from actual movegen/state code
- [x] Implement fixes for both issues
- [x] Full Rust workspace test suite green after fixes (`cargo test --workspace --offline`)
- [x] Sibling repo `hyperchess-live-stream` test suite green against the fixed `hyperchess-rules` dependency
- [x] Verify findings reported to user with file:line references and recommended fixes

## Outcomes / Summary

**Issue B (castling never occurs) — ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED, 100% reproducible.**
`crates/hyperchess-rules/src/core/piece_identity.rs`'s `START_HFEN_I` constant (the string
`Board::start_pos()` parses) encoded the castling field as `-` instead of `KQkq`. Since
`CastleRights::update_for_move` only ever clears bits and nothing ever sets them, every game
started from this constant began with zero castling rights and could never castle, regardless
of engine, skill, or seed — deterministic, not probabilistic, explaining "0 castling in 500k
games" exactly. The JS/TS layer's own hand-written starting-position literals
(`packages/board/src/web-component/hyperchess-board.ts`,
`packages/store/src/__tests__/game-store.test.ts`) already had the correct `KQkq`, confirming
this was an unintended Rust-side data bug, not a design choice. Fixed the constant; updated two
test fixtures that had baked in the wrong value (`hfen.rs::test_parse_start_hfen`,
`game.rs::test_save_hfeni_and_hpgni`) as expected, correct fallout. Full workspace test suite
(all crates) passes clean after the fix, including all perft/castling-specific tests — no
regressions.

**Issue A (grandmaster MCTS won't force mate, game runs to ply 1220+) — partial root cause found
and mitigated; full fix out of scope for one session.**
`hyperchess-search::mcts`'s CPU rollout scores non-terminal leaves via clamped static
material eval only (±3000cp → ±1.0). Once one side is hugely materially ahead, essentially
every legal reply saturates to the same clamped value, giving MCTS's UCB1 selection zero
gradient between "converging on mate" and aimless shuffling — a known weakness of
static-eval-rollout MCTS (no random/deep playout to an actual terminal state, no
mating-technique/mop-up term in the evaluator). Combined with `rule50` resetting on every pawn
push, the internal 224-halfmove no-progress draw can be deferred indefinitely while material
stays constant, letting a real game run far past the safety-net ply count if an external
`--max-moves`/ply cap isn't also tightly bounded.

Implemented a mating-technique shaping term (`mating_technique_bonus_cp` in
`crates/hyperchess-search/src/mcts.rs`) added to the CPU leaf eval before the existing clamp:
rewards driving a (near-)bare defending king toward a true corner (Manhattan-style
distance-from-centre, not Chebyshev — a max-based metric saturates the instant either axis
alone reaches the rim, which does not actually confine the king) and bringing the attacking
king closer, gated strictly to positions where the defender has under ~400cp of material so
ordinary middlegame/endgame play is untouched. Verified via new unit tests
(`mating_technique_bonus_*`, `leaf_score` tests) and empirically: an adversarial synthetic
14-piece-vs-lone-king position that failed to reach checkmate within 800 plies using a
Chebyshev-max version of the bonus converged at ply 713 once corrected to the
Manhattan-style metric. This is a real, verified improvement but not a complete fix — 700+
plies is still a long game for a "won" position; a full fix would need either shallow
tactical lookahead in the MCTS rollout instead of pure static eval, or a trained value
network (AlphaZero-style), both out of scope here. The CUDA/GPU MCTS path
(`hyperchess-search-cuda`) uses a separate kernel-based evaluator and was NOT touched — same
class of weakness likely applies there and is a documented follow-up.

**Secondary finding, not fixed:** `hyperchess-live-stream`'s
`crates/live-stream-gameplay/src/lib.rs::CoreEngineAdapter::select_move` (line ~266) never
uses `SkillProfile.search_time_cap_ms` — it calls `searcher.best_move(board, depth_guideline)`
with no wall-clock bound at all, so "Grandmaster"'s advertised 8-second cap is dead metadata.
Not fixed this session (didn't want to change live spectator-stream pacing behavior without
being asked, and this session can't verify against the live deployment) — flagged as an open
item.

**Pre-existing, unrelated issue found (not fixed):** `hyperchess-sim` fails to build against
current `hyrperchess-core` — `crates/../src/engine.rs:96` calls
`TimedSearcher::shredder_style()`, which does not exist in `hyperchess-search::timed` (only
`new`/`strategic`/`pro`/`with_profile`). Confirmed pre-existing (unrelated to this session's
diff — grep shows `shredder_style` never existed in `hyperchess-search`'s git history) via
`git stash` isolation. Left unfixed as out of scope; flagged for a separate session.
